            <ArticleTitle>The evolution of management of acute coronary syndromes (unstable angina and non-ST segment elevation myocardial infarction): Part l.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>143-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/clc.20392</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>As I advanced through medical school at Johns Hopkins, and throughout my subsequent training, I was constantly reminded of the contributions made to medicine by our predecessors. Many of whom were at Johns Hopkins, but many were not. As I continue active involvement in the education of medical students and physicians, it seems to me we have not done a good job of relating the history of how the modern management of patients with cardiovascular disease developed to our students, house staff, and fellows.</AbstractText>
